## Good behaviour — rules for working in a VCO-installed project

These rules apply to YOU (Claude) working inside any project that has VCO installed. They're independent of the project's own domain — keep them in mind regardless of whether you're coding a Rust web app, training an ML model, or writing documentation.

1. **Don't auto-destroy user data.** Weaviate collections, KG embeddings, code-graph embeddings, the launcher SQLite DB, and `.claude/state/` content represent hours of work and on-disk vectors that are expensive to regenerate. If your task seems to require dropping a collection, deleting `.claude/state/`, or wiping the launcher DB — STOP and ask the user explicitly. The orchestrator's own update flow uses a deferral pattern (`UPDATE_DEFERRED.md`) precisely so destructive operations are never auto-applied; follow the same discipline in any new code.
   - **Don't pollute it either, and an ad-hoc probe script is how that happens.** The pytest suite pins `WEAVIATE_URL` at the unroutable sentinel `http://127.0.0.1:9` and redirects the VCO state dirs; a bare `python /tmp/probe.py` gets none of that protection. Reproduce under `pytest` wherever you can; when a standalone harness is genuinely necessary, export `WEAVIATE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:9` (or point it at a disposable instance) BEFORE running it, and say in your report that you did. A write to a collection whose project stem is one of VCO's test-fixture names is refused unless the process sets `VCT_ALLOW_FIXTURE_CLASS_WRITES=1` — the guard exists because real knowledge nodes once landed in a fixture-named class that nothing reads.

2. **Don't delete bundled agents, skills, or hooks to "uninstall" them.** They are shipped by the orchestrator and `install-bundle --update` will re-add them. Disable them from the launcher GUI instead (per-project Agents / Skills / Hooks tabs → toggle off). Each tab's toggle edits the thing the harness actually reads, so know what it touches:
   - **Agents / Skills** — the file is MOVED between `.claude/agents/` and `.claude/agents.disabled/` (skills: the whole `.claude/skills/<name>/` directory). Claude Code discovers them by globbing those paths, so the move is the mechanism; nothing is deleted.
   - **Hooks** — the hook's entry is REMOVED from `.claude/settings.json` (the file Claude Code's hook engine reads) and parked in the launcher DB, so re-enabling restores it exactly and a bundle update does not put it back. That record lives in this machine's launcher DB only: on a copy or clone of the project on another machine, the next bundle update re-adds the hook. The hook SCRIPT file is never deleted.
   All three touch your working tree and `.claude/settings.json` is usually VCS-tracked, so expect these toggles to show up in `git status`.

3. **Don't bypass hooks or MCPs without telling the user.** If a hook fails or an MCP times out, surface the issue and let the user decide. Don't silently `export VCT_DISABLE_HOOKS=1` or strip a misbehaving server from `~/.claude.json` — those defaults were chosen for a reason, and silent overrides leak into other sessions.

4. **Don't write to `~/.claude.json` directly.** That file is the user's global Claude Code config. The orchestrator's install + update flows manage MCP registrations there; manual edits create drift. If you need to add an MCP, do it via `python install.py --update` (orchestrator root) or a per-project bundle update (see "Update flow" below), not by hand-editing the JSON.

5. **Respect the orchestrator vs project boundary.** `<orchestrator-root>/.claude/` is the orchestrator clone's own state (bundled agents, hooks, KG). `<your-project>/.claude/` is YOUR project's state. Don't write project-specific knowledge into the orchestrator clone, and don't edit orchestrator-shipped files inside a project (hooks, scripts, agents, skills): the next bundle update **adopts** them — your bytes are backed up to `.claude/backups/bundle-adoptions/<timestamp>/` and the shipped version is written. Only `knowledge/**` is exempt (it is your data and is always preserved). To change shipped behaviour, disable the item from the launcher or change the orchestrator source.

6. **Knowledge Graph writes should match scope.** Project-specific patterns → per-project KG (default `scope="project"`). Cross-project patterns that other projects on the same machine would benefit from → shared KG (`scope="shared"`). Don't pollute the shared KG with project-internal trivia; don't isolate genuinely reusable knowledge to a single project.

7. **When in doubt about an update flow, ask.** "Should I run `install.py --update`?" or "Should I drop the Weaviate collection to re-embed with the new model?" are reasonable questions to ask the user before acting — those operations take time and a wrong call costs the user more than the question.

8. **What VCO ships is exactly `templates/**` in the orchestrator repo — a `knowledge/` folder is never a shipping surface.** Every shipped artefact (hooks, scripts, agents, skills, settings, the curated knowledge set) is delivered from `templates/`; to know what VCO ships, read `templates/`, never a rendered or installed copy. The converse does not hold for knowledge:

   | | a project's `knowledge/` (the orchestrator root's included) | `templates/knowledge/` |
   |---|---|---|
   | what it is | private working notes | the curated set that SHIPS |
   | audience | that project's owner and their agents | every user of VCO |
   | how it grows | freely, as work happens | by a deliberate curation pass |

   A node present in a project's `knowledge/` and absent from `templates/knowledge/` is **the design, not a delivery gap**. Promoting a node into the shipped set is an owner decision, never a side effect of other work and never an agent's call — if you think a node deserves to ship, say so and stop.

