## Persistence layers: KG, context, memory, plans

Four layers carry state across sessions — future-you, future agents, and the user rely on them. Treat writes to them like commits to a codebase, not optional notes.

- **Knowledge Graph** (`knowledge/**/*.md`): patterns, concepts, decisions. Write a node BEFORE moving on after learning a non-obvious fact (architecture, gotcha, decision rationale, post-incident insight). The `kg-update-nudge` hook fires after ~175k work units accumulated without a KG write, then every 50k work units after (a constant interval — a second nudge means you skipped the first, not that the gap is growing); treat the nudge as a prompt to write, not noise. *Work units = output tokens + intake from Read/Web/Agent/Bash + file edits authored.* Three legitimate ways to silence it: (a) **write a real KG node** — `Write`/`Edit` to `knowledge/**/*.md` or `store_knowledge_node` (the default and what the nudge wants); (b) **transcript escape marker** `[No KG update needed: <one-line reason naming what you searched for>]` as top-level text in your reply (not inside a tool call) — only valid after running `hybrid_search` for 2-5 candidate lessons and finding nothing; the reason must name the topic(s) you searched (bare reasons like "nothing new" are rejected); (c) **env var `KG_NUDGE_OFF=1`** disables the hook for that shell. Use (a) by default, (b) when work was genuinely orthogonal (deploys, status reports, scrub-only), (c) only during release operations where injection would be noise.
- **`.claude/CONTEXT_STATE.md`** — active working memory (250–350 lines, max 500): current task, recent progress, next steps, active blockers. Update DURING work, not just at end — stale state means the next session starts confused. Unlike MEMORY.md it is never truncated: the `context-size-check.sh` hook emits a soft warning at 500 lines and never drops content.
  - **Per-session state (concurrent chats)**: `.claude/CONTEXT_STATE.md` is the shared project rollup. When you run multiple long-lived chats against the same project at once (e.g. a main chat + an RL chat), each can keep its OWN `.claude/context/CONTEXT_STATE_<session_id>.md` so they don't clobber the shared rollup (last-writer-wins). The size-check / diff-inject / compact-reinject hooks pick it up automatically once it exists; single-session projects never create one and pay nothing.
- **MEMORY.md** (`~/.claude/projects/.../memory/`) — your self-written operational notes: user preferences, recurring fixes, stable facts. Save corrections AND validations (don't just record what you got wrong). Not in git; edit via `/memory` or by asking directly. The memory feature loads it into the system prompt every session and hard-truncates to the first 200 lines / ~25 KB, silently dropping everything after (a harness behaviour, not configurable from VCO) — so keep it a concise one-line-per-entry index and put detail in topic files (`debugging.md`, etc.), which are loaded on demand and are not capped.
- **Plans** (`.claude/context/plans/`) — non-trivial work breakdowns with rationale; active plans live here, archived plans in `archive/`. Update when scope changes. `.claude/context/archive/` holds completed tasks.

| MEMORY.md | CONTEXT_STATE.md |
|---|---|
| "use pnpm not npm", solved recurring bugs, stable architecture facts, service URLs | Current sprint goal, recent progress ✅, next steps, open blockers |

**Concrete rule**: if you've done >2 hours of substantive work or learned a non-obvious thing, write the KG node BEFORE the user has to ask. Context and plans tend to get updated as work happens; the KG often does not — the hook backstops you, but the goal is to never need its nudge.

**Per-chat counter**: the nudge hook keys state by `session_id` in `kg_update_tokens.jsonl` under the metrics dir (`$VCT_STATE_DIR/metrics`, default `~/.vct/metrics`). Tokens from one Claude Code session do NOT leak into another — each conversation has its own counter.

**Token efficiency**: batch parallel tool calls (Read/Grep multiple files in a single message); read files <150 lines directly and use `offset`/`limit` for larger ones; do not re-read a file already in context; limit shell output (`| head -30` or `2>&1 | tail -20`); skip echoing after writes — trust the tool result; spawn agents for multi-file ops (cap 3 concurrent EDITORS per shared tree; read-only agents may run wider). For large documents (>20 pages), skim the ToC/structure first, then do targeted section reads.

