## Agents & Skills

The installer drops a default set of agents into `.claude/agents/` and skills into `.claude/skills/`. Customize freely — they are templates, not framework code.

**Invoke skills**: `/skill-name` (e.g. `/fix-issue`, `/task-breakdown`).
**Spawn agents**: `@agent-name (Model)` via the Agent tool. (`Task` is a legacy alias for `Agent`.)

**Model selection.** Name the model on every subagent dispatch — via the Agent-tool `model` parameter or `model:` in the agent definition's frontmatter. Never rely on a subagent inheriting the session's model: an unnamed dispatch makes "which model wrote this?" depend on ambient state. When several sources name a model, the resolution order is: Agent-tool `model` parameter > definition frontmatter > `CLAUDE_CODE_SUBAGENT_MODEL` env var > session model. `/tasks` shows the model each subagent actually ran on.

VCO itself never sets `CLAUDE_CODE_SUBAGENT_MODEL`, nor any Claude tier slot (`ANTHROPIC_DEFAULT_{OPUS,SONNET,HAIKU,FABLE}_MODEL`, `ANTHROPIC_SMALL_FAST_MODEL`) — not even when it points the VS Code panel at a model gateway; an override already present in your settings is preserved and reported, never silently honoured. The one edit VCO makes is appending the `[1m]` context-window hint to the SAME model id, disclosed in the done message — the model a slot names is never changed. The rule behind that is absolute: **a model name must name the model that answers.** Dispatching a lane under one name while another model answers makes attribution permanently unanswerable. Choose models visibly — `/model`, or `model:` in the agent's frontmatter — never through an ambient default.

{{MODEL_SELECTION_GRID}}

**When to Spawn**:
- Task parallel to current work.
- Requires sustained focus (30+ min).
- Isolated context.
- Substantial output (>200 lines).

**Don't Spawn**:
- <5 min tasks.
- Needs immediate back-and-forth.
- Exploring/brainstorming.

**Handoff Format** (300–500 tokens):

```
@agent-name (Model)
Task: One sentence goal
Context: File paths, patterns, constraints
Success Criteria: What "done" looks like
Output: Where to save
```

**Parallel agents on the same git repo**: when spawning 2+ agents that will edit the same working tree, either give each one an isolated git worktree (Agent-tool `isolation: "worktree"`, or instruct the agent to `git worktree add` and work there) OR assign strictly disjoint file sets and forbid ALL git commands in their briefs (the coordinator runs git). Concurrent `git checkout` / `stash` / staging operations are tree-global and clobber each other — that is the failure the rule prevents. The cap of 3 concurrent agents applies to agents that EDIT the same tree; read-only agents (audits, searches) and agents on different repos may fan out wider.

**Waiting on long-running work**: when you need to wait for something to finish (a test suite, a build, a background agent), start it with `run_in_background` and continue or end your turn — its completion notification wakes you when it is done.

**Frontmatter**: the full field lists live in the vendor docs — https://code.claude.com/docs/en/sub-agents and https://code.claude.com/docs/en/skills (a copy here drifts from the harness). The fields that change how VCO work gets dispatched:
- `model` (agents and skills) — a tier alias (`opus`, `sonnet`, `haiku`, `fable`, `inherit`) or a full model id. Gateway ids (`claude-gw/<id>`) work in a definition's frontmatter; the Agent tool's own model enum cannot carry them, so dispatch a gateway agent by definition name and pass no model override.
- `effort` (agents) — caps per-agent reasoning cost. Settable only in frontmatter, not as an Agent-tool parameter; `CLAUDE_CODE_EFFORT_LEVEL` overrides frontmatter globally. Brief ad-hoc agents at `medium` effort — `medium` is the right default for substantive work (implementation, refactors, multi-file changes, design + code); reserve `high` for tasks that genuinely need it (hard debugging, security review, cross-subsystem design) and treat it as the ceiling. `xhigh` and `max` are not used for subagents — models that do not expose extended thinking reject `xhigh` outright, so pinning it makes the agent fail to start rather than reason harder. Every bundled agent ships at `medium`; the few kept at `high` are genuinely hard-reasoning roles (deep research, incident response, adversarial review). Trivial sweeps stay at default.
- `disable-model-invocation: true` (skills) — only the user can invoke via `/command`; `user-invocable: false` — hide from the `/` menu, only Claude auto-invokes; `context: fork` — run in an isolated subagent context (CLI only).
- `isolation: worktree`, `background: true`, `memory`, `skills`, `mcpServers`, `hooks`, `tools` / `disallowedTools`, `maxTurns`, `permissionMode` — scoping and lifecycle fields; see the vendor docs.

